llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
131): on gfx803, matmuls with fp16 weights that go to cuBLAS (hipBLAS) ask
for fp32 compute instead of fp16.
gfx803-specific: engages only for cc == GGML_CUDA_CC_GCN4.

WHY
---
ggml_cuda_mul_mat_cublas() runs a matmul with fp16 weights (the vision
projector of an image model, any F16 GGUF) in fp16 compute on every AMD GPU
except CDNA and RDNA4, because fast_fp16_hardware_available() is true for
all of them. hipBLAS passes that to rocBLAS as gemm_ex with an fp16 compute
type, which the base image's SGEMM shim does not take, and rocBLAS runs it
on Tensile kernels that accumulate in fp16 (Cijk_Alik_Bljk_HB_*). Against the
CPU backend these products carry a normalized squared error of 2-9e-5 (about
1% RMS) at K = 1152-4304, the shapes of Qwen3.5's and Qwen3-VL's vision
encoder, against 4e-8 for the same product accumulated in fp32 and stored as
fp16. They also run at about 3.3 TFLOPS. gfx803 has no packed fp16 math, so
fp16 accumulation buys it no speed: a 1080p image's 112 encoder projections
took 2.0 s of Qwen3.5 9B's 16 s of GPU time on those kernels.

WHAT
----
On GCN4 the call keeps fp16 operands and an fp16 product but asks for fp32
compute, with fp32 alpha and beta. The base image's SGEMM shim takes
all-fp16 gemm_ex and gemm_strided_batched_ex calls with fp32 compute and runs
them on its fp16 tiles, which accumulate in fp32. Without the shim, rocBLAS
computes the call with the same accuracy, but more slowly than either
(3456 x 8160 x 1152: 24.7 ms, against 20.0 on the fp16-compute kernels and
15.5 on the shim). The product is still stored as fp16 and widened to fp32
afterwards, as before.

Result on an RX 590, test-backend-ops (f16 weights, f32 activations):

    MUL_MAT f16 cases (229)    NMSE against the CPU geometric mean 0.008x
                               on the 114 that take this path, largest
                               9.2e-5 -> 4.3e-8; all pass before and after

    ms per call                       n = 512          n = 8160
    3456 x 1152 (qkv)              1.49 -> 0.94     19.95 -> 15.51
    1152 x 1152 (out)              0.55 -> 0.36      6.90 ->  5.34
    4304 x 1152 (fc1)              1.93 -> 1.29     27.04 -> 19.38
    1152 x 4304 (fc2)              1.95 -> 1.27     28.00 -> 19.17

A 1080p image prompt (2,061 tokens) on Qwen3.5 9B with its vision
projector, llama-server: 14.19 -> 13.61 s.

Verified on an RX 590: test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o MUL_MAT and
-o MUL_MAT_ID pass, with the image's shim preloaded; only the fp16-weight
cases change. To reproduce, build llama.cpp b11232 with GGML_HIP=ON and
AMDGPU_TARGETS=gfx803, then with and without the patch:

    test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o MUL_MAT -p type_a=f16
        (largest NMSE against the CPU per case)
    llama-server -m <Qwen3.5 9B> --mmproj <its F16 projector> -c 16384
        -ngl 99 -fa on
        (send a 1080p image; the response's timings give the prompt time)

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
index b8be34b..b07d408 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
@@ -1541,6 +1541,16 @@ static void ggml_cuda_mul_mat_cublas_impl(ggml_backend_cuda_context & ctx, const
         prefer_f32_output = !GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_RDNA3(cc) && !GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_CDNA(cc);
     }
 
+    // gfx803 has no packed fp16 math, so fp16 accumulation buys no speed, and rocBLAS's fp16-compute
+    // kernels (Tensile HB) lose precision over long sums and run at about 3.3 TFLOPS. With fp32
+    // compute the same fp16 GEMM runs on the gfx803 SGEMM that the image preloads, which accumulates
+    // in fp32. The product is still stored as fp16 and widened below.
+    if (compute_type == GGML_TYPE_F16 && cc == GGML_CUDA_CC_GCN4 && !prefer_f32_output) {
+        cu_compute_type = batched_mul_mat_traits<GGML_TYPE_F32>::compute_type;
+        alpha = batched_mul_mat_traits<GGML_TYPE_F32>::get_alpha();
+        beta  = batched_mul_mat_traits<GGML_TYPE_F32>::get_beta();
+    }
+
     if (prefer_f32_output) {
         dst_ptr = (char *) dst_ddf;
         cu_compute_type = batched_mul_mat_traits<GGML_TYPE_F32>::compute_type;
